WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.680
So my name is Nic Crane.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.050
I'm a software engineer
at Voltron Data,

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.510
and I'm also one of the
maintainers on the Apache Arrow

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:16.410
project, and I work
mainly on the R package.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.210
So I started learning R
about 12 years ago.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.810
And I just got so excited
about the idea of open source.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.860
I went from being like this
passive consumer of software

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.770
to now kind of having this thing
that I could kind of pull apart

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.340
and it was like
seeing the matrix,

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.540
seeing inside kind of all these
functions and things like that.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.670
But despite that being
quite a while ago,

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.980
it took me about six
more years, I'd say,

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.350
to really start to connect
with the R communities

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.850
and another few
years on top of that

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.680
to make my first contribution.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.450
So what I want to talk
to you about today

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.117
is kind of the things
that got in my way

00:00:47.117 --> 00:00:49.170
and stop me getting
involved with contributing

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.530
to open source a bit sooner when
I probably actually could have.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.170
And actually, like, it's
been so beneficial to me,

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.510
I almost wish I had sooner.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.870
But first, so in the way
that everybody does science,

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.430
I wanted to do a Twitter poll.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.920
So question for my
rstats folks who have

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.440
contributed to open source.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.850
How many years have you been
using open source libraries

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.255
for before you made
your first contribution?

00:01:11.255 --> 00:01:13.630
And actually, it was pretty
easy interesting to see that.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.080
It was pretty nice to
see that for most people,

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.815
pretty soon, actually,
two to three years

00:01:17.815 --> 00:01:20.190
of using open source libraries
before they kind of really

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:21.390
got involved.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.580
But actually, it's
quite a lot of people

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.920
in a similar situation
to where I was where

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.050
six or more years before they
really of made that first leap.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:32.850
So I'm going to start
off just by talking

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.520
about why contribute to open
source and the benefits it's

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:36.480
had for me.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:37.980
But before I go
into that, I just

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:39.680
want to start with
a bit of disclaimer.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.150
Like, I'm not saying that
contributing to open source

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.100
is necessary to be a
good software engineer

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.280
or a good data scientist.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:46.330
It's absolutely not.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.180
I think there's a certain amount
of privilege being involved

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:51.130
with even having the time to do
so alongside everything else.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:53.820
So that's not what
I'm going for here.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.010
But going back to
what I've gotten out

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.410
of contributing
to open source, I

00:01:58.410 --> 00:01:59.970
feel like it's been
the best learning

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.670
experience of my
career and definitely

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.670
kind of contributing kind
of code and non code things

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.650
and getting feedback
from people has

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.690
been the biggest acceleration
of my development

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.640
as a software engineer.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.140
Working in public is great.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.370
You know, I figure
out something that's

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.870
taken me ages and I blog about
it and then somebody is like,

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.890
OK, I get it now,
really rewarding.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.420
And again, it's great to
be part of a community.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:24.630
And I think as well,
it's great to have

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:25.985
that proof of your skills.

00:02:25.985 --> 00:02:28.110
Like, I'm not the most
necessarily confident person

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.480
as a software engineer, but
having those sort of bragging

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.580
rights, you know, like I've
kind of made those contributions

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:33.620
to that project.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.340
OK, well, I guess I
must know what I'm

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.750
doing some of the time, then.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.060
And I'd also just
like giving back

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.790
to projects that have
helped me in my work before.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:43.907
OK, so that's kind
of the benefit,

00:02:43.907 --> 00:02:45.990
but what are the kind of
things that kind of maybe

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.570
got in my way or some
quite common complaints?

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.593
So like I think one of the
flip sides of open source

00:02:51.593 --> 00:02:53.010
being this kind
of massive network

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.350
of self-governing
communities is that it

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.860
has been quite kind
of OK in some bits

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.620
to have kind of quite strong
personalities and people kind

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.800
of not behaving amazingly,
in some bits anyway.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.550
So I think I definitely
had that question.

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:10.170
You know, are people going
to be friendly or mean?

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.160
There's also the question
of not knowing how.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.847
So I was at a point where I
could write functions and even

00:03:14.847 --> 00:03:16.680
write R packages, but
I'd not made a pull

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.130
request or a project yet.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:21.200
Other questions are things
like, what if I don't

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.530
have the skills to do this?

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.120
What if I don't
know where to start?

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.150
And even just that fear that
working in public is scary.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.450
I get to share my
successes, but I'm also

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:32.590
kind of sharing
my failures a bit.

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:35.740
So just to get kind of one
barrier out of the way,

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.930
if it is literally
just the process of how

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:39.670
do I make a pull
request to a project

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:42.400
that is stopping you from
contributing to open source,

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.920
I'd say please do check
out the first contributions

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:48.140
repo that's kind of
looked after by forwards.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.930
So I was involved in kind of
making the R version of this.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.750
And basically, it takes
you through the process

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:54.670
of making a pull request.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:57.640
So if you already a bit of Git
and know how to use GitHub,

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.290
that is all you
need to kind of just

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:03.050
walk through the kind of
mechanical steps for it.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:05.550
But actually I think the things
that really hold people back

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:08.280
from getting involved aren't kind
of the actual process things,

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.280
and it's more these kind
of concerns or worries.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:12.360
So the main bulk of
this talk is going

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.370
to be basically about
things that I now know

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.130
that I wish I'd known before.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.320
So the first big question
I had is, how to pick,

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.720
where to get involved
and what to work on.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.780
And I think this is just really
complicated by maybe the view

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.460
that I had of open source.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.740
And I really had this kind
of slightly misguided view

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:29.320
at first

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.470
that open source is
one big community,

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:32.530
and that's just not true.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.250
There are lots of open source
sub-communities and even

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.820
within languages -- within
R we see this right.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.950
But I think even
kind of stepping back

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.930
from that kind of my
next view of open source

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.260
wasn't quite right either.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.170
So to kind of illustrate it.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:49.050
So this is a screenshot
from the movie Mean Girls.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.180
Now, if you've not seen
that, like I'm sure

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:54.077
everybody at some point has
seen some TV show or movie

00:04:54.077 --> 00:04:56.160
where you've got this kind
of American high school

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.128
cafeteria and the new
kid's walking through.

00:04:58.128 --> 00:04:59.670
And it's like, are these
people going

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:01.450
to let me sit at the
table or are they not?

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:03.242
And that feeling of,
you know, these people

00:05:03.242 --> 00:05:05.610
are kind of mean and these
people are kind of nice,

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:06.703
and things like that.

00:05:06.703 --> 00:05:08.370
And this metaphor
completely breaks down

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.740
because everybody is nerds here.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:13.498
But the point was viewing
it as these people

00:05:13.498 --> 00:05:14.290
are mean and awful.

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:15.457
And these people are lovely

00:05:15.457 --> 00:05:16.860
over here isn't quite right.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.620
And I think with a lot of
projects it's easy to forget.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.370
Like a lot of projects are
kind of staffed by volunteers

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.710
and there's a lot of reasons
why kind of some projects

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:27.352
just don't want
new contributors.

00:05:27.352 --> 00:05:29.310
So they might be going
through a design phase,

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.950
they might just be really
small and it does take effort

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:33.490
to onboard a new contributor.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:36.970
So I kind of came to
realize that is OK.

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:39.570
But then of course, like the
next obvious question is, so

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:42.270
if some projects don't
want new contributors,

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.795
then how do I find
the ones that do?

00:05:44.795 --> 00:05:46.170
And actually, that
was way easier

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:50.130
to answer than I thought.
So, they'll tell you.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:53.910
So, for example, this is an
issue from the Arrow project.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:56.355
This is one that we've
labeled good first issue

00:05:56.355 --> 00:05:58.230
to say, you know, this
is a good place to get

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.707
started getting involved.

00:05:59.707 --> 00:06:02.040
And again, similarly, here's
I think this is an old one,

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.350
but this is from dplyr,
and it's been labeled

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:06.630
both good first issue and
help wanted, like clear

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:09.900
signaling like we want
people to get involved here.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.158
I will say like projects
that don't have these labels,

00:06:12.158 --> 00:06:14.200
it doesn't mean they don't
want new contributors.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.617
But if this is a
concern of yours,

00:06:15.617 --> 00:06:19.170
this isn't kind of an
easy way to figure it out.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.210
So the next question
I guess then I had

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.130
is what to contribute.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:25.080
And I think commonly
given advice

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:27.870
that I think is good advice is
all about starting small, build

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:28.830
up trust.

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.520
Show people you've
read the guidelines.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:32.910
And you can kind of
go from small things

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:34.410
and bigger from there.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.630
I think one thing that was
really important for me,

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:39.090
though, actually,
is it is way easier

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:42.030
to contribute to packages
that you use yourself.

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:44.115
So my first contribution
was to the broom package

00:06:44.115 --> 00:06:45.490
and that went well
and everything.

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:47.975
But there were points during
that kind of code contribution

00:06:47.975 --> 00:06:50.100
where I was like, I don't
know if I can change this

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.560
because I wasn't a regular
user of the package,

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.810
and it would have been much
easier if it was something

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:57.030
that I had a bit more kind
of day to day use with.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:00.155
And of course, like if you found
problems in packages or bugs

00:07:00.155 --> 00:07:01.530
that you've
experienced yourself,

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:03.480
that's a really great
place to get involved,

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.022
kind of figuring
out what's going on,

00:07:05.022 --> 00:07:07.710
fixing it, submitting
a pull request. Equally

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.640
with documentation
you find unclear.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:11.400
I feel like if you're
newer to something,

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:13.030
you're actually
an advantage here.

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:15.150
Like you're coming to
this problem with kind

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:17.153
of like a fresh
mindset and therefore

00:07:17.153 --> 00:07:19.320
like if the documentation
doesn't make sense to you,

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:21.612
it's probably not going to
make sense to somebody else.

00:07:21.612 --> 00:07:25.470
And that's a really nice
early contribution to make.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:27.960
And just in general, if you've
got a specific maybe area

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:29.850
that you've got expertise,
domain knowledge,

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.100
that's also a great place
to go and kind of like

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.960
use that if it's a package
that deals with that.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:38.430
OK so I think for
me, the really,

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:41.460
really, really big question
about how to get involved

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.977
was how to navigate
social dynamics.

00:07:43.977 --> 00:07:45.810
And this is a really
tricky one because, as I

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:47.880
said before, every
project is different

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:49.980
and made up of quite
different people,

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:52.143
even kind of within
the same project.

00:07:52.143 --> 00:07:54.310
Now there's this quote that
I really like, actually.

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:57.370
So this is a book called
Working in Public by Nadia Eghbal.

00:07:57.370 --> 00:07:59.652
And if you're interested
in open source governance

00:07:59.652 --> 00:08:02.110
and how these things work, I
would definitely recommend it.

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:02.890
Really good book.

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:06.780
But in the book, Nadia says
open source is complicated

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:09.540
because it contains a messy mix
of both technical and social

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:11.903
norms, most of which
play out in public.

00:08:11.903 --> 00:08:13.320
And I think this
really gets to it

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.780
for me, like it's
figuring out how

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:18.030
to kind of change
something, make a function,

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:19.120
and that's one thing.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:22.170
But just figuring out how to be
this new person in this group

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:26.070
and go out and not
annoy people and kind

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.710
of just get things done
that can be really tricky.

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:32.169
So one of the ways of
overcoming this, I think,

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:34.059
is a lot of projects
have participation,

00:08:34.059 --> 00:08:37.630
have kind of contributing
guidelines and documentation.

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:40.585
So I this is kind of like
a screenshot of a page

00:08:40.585 --> 00:08:41.710
that I really, really like,

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:44.049
actually. It's the Apache
Software Foundation

00:08:44.049 --> 00:08:46.660
kind of member
participation guidelines.

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:49.630
And it goes into detail
even the minute detail

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:52.540
of how to kind of
phrase requests

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:56.650
kind of make significant changes
to make it kind of as, I guess,

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:58.780
as smoothly as possible.

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:01.630
I say this doesn't just apply
to Apache Software Foundation

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:03.610
projects, and it's
definitely worth kind

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:05.830
of skimming through these
if that's kind of a concern

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:07.950
that you have yourself.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.630
But then the other
thing here I think

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:12.330
is like sometimes just
embracing imposter syndrome,

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.600
like you are new to this
thing and that's great.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.100
But like if you are pretending
to be somebody that was there

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:18.960
already, what would you do?

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:20.610
So the answer could
be can go and read

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.793
previous pull requests
and previous issues

00:09:22.793 --> 00:09:24.710
and look at the kind of
things that people do.

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:26.520
So like every
different package is

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:27.870
going to have something
slightly different

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:30.245
and it's always good just to
see what the conventions are

00:09:30.245 --> 00:09:31.740
and to follow them.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:33.450
I will say the one
thing to kind of add

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:35.790
to that is, as a new contributor,
it's sometimes better

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:38.190
to err on the side
of over communicating

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:40.710
rather than under communicating
because an established kind

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:43.020
of contributor might
kind of need to say less

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:44.290
to get something accepted.

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:47.010
But still, I found reading
those previous PRs

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:50.020
and issues has been
super, super useful.

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:53.560
OK now, this is a
really weird one for me.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:54.975
Something I've noticed.

00:09:54.975 --> 00:09:56.350
And again, this
is very different

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:58.810
between different projects,
but definitely on some.

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:01.360
There's this real
difference in social norms

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.910
and quite stark
differences between what

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:07.630
would come across a certain way
in real life versus kind of how

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:09.430
things look on a pull request.

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:12.400
So to step back a second,
we've got these projects that

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:15.400
have maybe got busy maintainers,
lots of different issues,

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:18.482
and there's a certain level of
bluntness that can appear rude,

00:10:18.482 --> 00:10:20.440
but is people just trying
to get through issues

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:22.440
and just trying to get
as much done as possible.

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.700
And I've definitely had
to kind of come to terms

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:27.790
with having this real
duality of maybe seeing

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:29.770
that kind of feedback
and getting it myself

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:32.860
and not taking it as rude or
personal or anything like that

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:34.280
and just seeing
it for what it is.

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:35.890
But at the same
time, actually not

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:38.650
necessarily engaging in that
communication style too much

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:39.942
myself because I don't like it.

00:10:39.942 --> 00:10:41.483
And just because
it's a normal thing,

00:10:41.483 --> 00:10:42.950
it doesn't mean I have to do it.

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:47.660
But again, I can still exist
in that world and that is fine.

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:50.740
So my third question
then was what

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:53.240
does the process of getting a
pull request accepted actually

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:54.082
look like?

00:10:54.082 --> 00:10:55.790
And this is another
one where I've really

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:58.760
had to kind of change
my, I guess, the way

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:00.330
that I view these
kind of things.

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:03.530
So previously I kind of
viewed it a bit like an essay.

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:05.720
So you kind of you're in
school, you do an essay,

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:08.720
you do your work, you check
it, you hand it in to somebody,

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:10.605
and then you wait a
bit and you get it back

00:11:10.605 --> 00:11:12.230
and you've got some
kind of mark that's

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:15.188
an indication of your
skill or competence.

00:11:15.188 --> 00:11:17.480
That is really not a good
way to look at a pull request.

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:19.760
Like, you will have a
bad time if you do that.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:22.070
And the reason for that
is like a pull request

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:24.470
is more like a
conversation than an essay.

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:27.110
Like, the back and forth and
suggestions for changing things

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:28.590
and taking a different approach,

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:31.160
that is part of why one of
the best things about getting

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:34.082
involved in open source, that
is when you are really learning,

00:11:34.082 --> 00:11:36.290
that is when you're getting
feedback from people that

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:40.080
are probably quite experienced
at this to improve things.

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:42.933
So, yeah, definitely not
treating like an essay.

00:11:42.933 --> 00:11:45.350
And I think another thing kind
of around this kind of pull

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:48.470
request kind of
approach as well is

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:50.030
I found that coming
from a background

00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:51.985
where I've been working
in kind of consultancy

00:11:51.985 --> 00:11:53.360
and a bit of
software engineering

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:57.020
on proprietary projects,
working on open source libraries

00:11:57.020 --> 00:12:01.190
is a completely different thing
in some really subtle ways.

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:03.770
And this is something that I
summarize as a task oriented

00:12:03.770 --> 00:12:06.320
versus code oriented approach.

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:09.020
So when I was a consultant,
I would be working,

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:11.090
building things with libraries.

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:12.957
So the focus there is
getting tasks done.

00:12:12.957 --> 00:12:14.540
You know, you might
be building an app

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:16.665
for a user that needs to
do a certain thing as part

00:12:16.665 --> 00:12:18.590
of their day job.

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:20.800
You might have a known set
of users, or at least some

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:21.300
of them,

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:22.842
you could maybe go
sit down with them

00:12:22.842 --> 00:12:24.962
and see how they'd
normally do that task.

00:12:24.962 --> 00:12:26.420
And it makes it a
bit easier really

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:28.220
to kind of see what
they're likely to do

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:29.637
and what they're
not likely to do.

00:12:29.637 --> 00:12:31.790
And you can kind of get
away with only testing

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:33.830
kind of like certain paths.

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:36.560
In these circumstances,
like dependencies

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:37.980
can be a lot less important.

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:41.150
Like you can add new packages
and it not be a big deal

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:42.650
and you might be
the only maintainer

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:43.770
or part of a small team.

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:45.300
So you can kind of do things

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:47.130
however you want as
long as it works.

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:49.130
But this is like
completely different now,

00:12:49.130 --> 00:12:51.200
like working on open
source libraries,

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:53.840
like your users in your
tasks are mostly unknown,

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:55.640
which means you have
to be really rigorous

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:58.430
with your testing and even
kind of preventing paths

00:12:58.430 --> 00:12:59.930
that you think no
one will ever take,

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:02.513
or testing things that you think
no one will ever use actually

00:13:02.513 --> 00:13:04.320
does become quite important.

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:06.990
There's often kind
of a need to really avoid

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:09.510
adding extra dependencies
here, but that

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:11.080
can lead to really good things.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:12.927
So I think sometimes
it can be easy to say,

00:13:12.927 --> 00:13:15.010
right, I can't get this
working with this package.

00:13:15.010 --> 00:13:16.780
Let's just use this other
one or a bit from both.

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:19.200
But when you're working on
kind of open source libraries,

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:20.610
you kind of can't do that.

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:23.550
But that forces you then to
learn to work with the tools

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:25.290
that you've got in
front of you and learn

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:28.120
that kind of last 10% of a
library to get a task done.

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:30.120
And I think, again, that's
been something that's

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:32.712
been really valuable for me.

00:13:32.712 --> 00:13:34.170
And also just this
idea that you're

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.580
writing code that isn't
kind of your code.

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.370
This is to be maintained
by kind of maybe

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:40.890
yourself but other
people and the community.

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:43.260
And that means you
can't have these maybe

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:46.553
weird idiosyncrasies
or kind of code smells.

00:13:46.553 --> 00:13:47.970
You've got to be
a bit more strict

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:49.410
about these kind of things.

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:51.660
And I think just
kind of realizing

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:53.580
that it's a really
different kind of mindset

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:56.490
between these two things
kind of makes it easier

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:59.400
to accept what kind of might
seem like pedantry sometimes,

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:03.330
but it's actually like
for a real good purpose.

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:06.720
But actually, like this isn't
even the right question.

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:09.300
Like, you could submit a
pull request to a project

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:11.320
and it might not get accepted.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:12.880
And that could be a good thing.

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:13.900
It could, I don't know.

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:15.570
You could try to implement
something that doesn't quite

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:17.160
work, but it exposes
something else

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:19.950
in the package that actually
needs changing and updating.

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:21.840
And that's a helpful
contribution, right?

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:25.593
You figured something out that
can make this software better.

00:14:25.593 --> 00:14:27.010
And actually, I
think the question

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:29.945
should be like, how do I make
a meaningful contribution?

00:14:29.945 --> 00:14:31.570
Because actually when
I think about it,

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:33.263
I feel really good
about the code

00:14:33.263 --> 00:14:35.680
that I've kind of contributed
towards kind of Apache Arrow

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:36.940
and stuff like that.

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:39.550
But actually I feel like some
of my most meaningful open source

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:41.885
contributions have
nothing to do with code.

00:14:41.885 --> 00:14:44.260
Like I love writing blog post
when I've learned something

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:46.150
that I found difficult, and
then somebody else is like,

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:47.525
yeah, no, that
makes sense to me

00:14:47.525 --> 00:14:50.560
now, finally. Like, I love
improving documentation

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:52.750
and taking that kind
of beginner's mindset.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:55.630
I even like tweeting about
a bit of functionality

00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:58.270
that is like not well advertised
and somebody suddenly like,

00:14:58.270 --> 00:15:00.380
I can now achieve
something with that.

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:03.400
I feel like that's
kind of meaningful.

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:07.000
So to summarize, then, 
open source is about people.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:09.470
There isn't a single
open source community.

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:12.310
There's a lot of different sub
communities within the things,

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:15.268
and there's no one size
fits all approach to saying,

00:15:15.268 --> 00:15:16.810
you should do this
here or you should

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:18.290
behave like that in that way.

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:21.250
It's about just figuring it
out for the thing that you're

00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:23.620
actually looking at the time.

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:26.080
I think especially maybe as
a new, more nervous person,

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:28.330
like just find communities
that you want to be part of

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:30.390
and want you to be part of them.

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:33.282
Also just remember
everybody is new at some point.

00:15:33.282 --> 00:15:34.740
And I think that
once you have done

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:36.570
the work of reading
the guidelines

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:39.047
and kind of a few previous
pull requests and approaches,

00:15:39.047 --> 00:15:41.130
like after that, there's
a point at which you just

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:43.770
have to jump right in and
go for it, and kind of then

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:46.930
welcome that kind of
code review conversation.

00:15:46.930 --> 00:15:48.720
But as much as I'm
talking about code now,

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:50.190
like open source
contributions are

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:53.100
about way more than just
code, and that is definitely

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:55.320
something to keep in mind.

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:57.230
Thank you.

